.SH NAME
help \- ask for help
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B help
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Help\^
finds information to explain a message from a command or explain 
the use of a command.
Zero or more arguments may be supplied.
If no arguments are given,
.B help\^
will prompt for one.
.PP
The arguments may be either message numbers (which normally appear
in parentheses following messages) or command names, of one of
the following types:
.PP
.TP 10
type 1
Begins with non-numerics, ends in numerics.
The non-numeric prefix is usually an abbreviation for the 
program or set of routines which produced the message
(e.g., \fBge6\fP, for message 6 from the
.B get\^
command).
.TP 10
type 2
Does not contain numerics (as a command, such as
.BR get )
.TP 10
type 3
Is all numeric (e.g., \fB212\fP)
.PP
The response of the program will be the explanatory information 
related to the argument, if there is any.
.PP
When all else fails, try "help stuck."
.SH FILES
.PP
.TP 20
/usr/lib/help
directory containing files of message text
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Use
.BR help (1)
for explanations.
.SH SEE ALSO
man(1), INTRO(1), apropos(1).
